## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical webhook security skills: verifying integrity, preventing replay, and tying verification to a realistic, low-friction workflow.

## Key Concepts
- Webhook signature verification (HMAC SHA-256)
- Timestamp tolerance and replay protection
- Nonce/idempotency storage (Redis or in-memory with TTL)
- Secret rotation and basic testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express middleware sketch (verify signature)
const crypto = require('crypto')
function verifyWebhook(req, res, next) {
  const sig = req.headers['x-signature']
  const body = JSON.stringify(req.body)
  const expected = crypto.createHmac('sha256', process.env.WEBHOOK_SECRET)
    .update(body)
    .digest('hex')
  if (sig !== expected) return res.status(400).send('Invalid signature')
  const ts = parseInt(req.headers['x-timestamp']||'0')
  if (Date.now()/1000 - ts > 300) return res.status(400).send('Timestamp too old')
  // store nonce to prevent replay (pseudo-code)
  const nonce = req.headers['x-webhook-nonce']
  if (await redis.setnx(`wn:${nonce}`, '1') === 0) return res.status(409).send('Replay detected')
  await redis.expire(`wn:${nonce}`, 300)
  next()
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle multiple providers with different secrets?
- How would you simulate and test replay protection in CI?
- What are trade-offs of using Redis vs in-memory storage for nonce tracking?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical security fundamentals at junior level with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Rate limiting and lockout; constant-time comparisons; password hashing; MFA basics; secure logging/privacy.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo login flow illustrating bcrypt compare, MFA, and session issuance
async function login(req, res) {
  const { username, password, mfaToken } = req.body;
  const user = await getUser(username);
  if (!user) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.hash);
  if (!ok) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  if (user.mfaEnabled) {
    if (!mfaToken) return res.status(401).send('MFA required');
    const verified = speakeasy.totp.verify({ secret: user.mfaSecret, encoding: 'base32', token: mfaToken });
    if (!verified) return res.status(401).send('Invalid MFA');
  }
  const session = createSession(user.id);
  res.cookie('session', session, { httpOnly: true, secure: true });
  return res.send({ ok: true });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for distributed rate limiting?
- How would you test lockout and recovery flows?
- How would you store and rotate MFA seeds securely?

